tools/

# wf_0005's parser-recovery agent writes these fresh every time the offline sequence (or a live
# run) recovers it: an extension under scripts/parsers/ext/ and its regression fixture under
# tests/parser_corpus/ (replayed verbatim from samples/wf_0005/canned/parser-recovery/ by
# MockRunner). Deliberately excluded, not accidental (Task 17): committing them would let
# scripts/parse.py auto-load the extension on every future run (parse.py's DEFAULT_EXT_DIR always
# loads scripts/parsers/ext/), so wf_0005 would parse clean on the first attempt from then on and
# stop exercising the parser-recovery path it exists to test -- see
# samples/wf_0005/canned/parser-recovery/tests/parser_corpus/acme_dedupe/README.md and
# docs/superpowers/build-reports/2026-09-18-pipeline/task-17-report.md.
/scripts/parsers/ext/acme_dedupe.py
/tests/parser_corpus/acme_dedupe/

# dbt run artefacts: validate_dbt.py keeps its own console logs under dbt/logs/ (git-ignored on
# purpose, task B); everything else here is what a human's own `dbt run`/`dbt parse` would write --
# never committed, and never read back by any script.
workflows/*/dbt/logs/
workflows/*/dbt/target/
workflows/*/dbt/dbt_packages/
workflows/*/dbt/.user.yml

# the orchestrator's `--runner external` hands each agent call out through <root>/.agent-requests/ (never
# committed), `deploy.py --render` writes build.rendered.sql beside the build file (a generated copy with the
# schema names filled in), and `.worktrees/` holds this repository's own linked git worktrees.
.agent-requests/
workflows/*/build.rendered.sql
.worktrees/
